// =============================================================================
// LAKEFLOW CONNECT DIRECT CDC FROM ON-PREMISES SQL SERVER / ORACLE, THROUGH A
// PRIVATE NETWORK GATEWAY (AZURE)
//
// JSONC for you to read; emit PLAIN JSON (strip the // lines) when you write
// architecture.md. Read reference/style-networking.md first; the network part is the
// same as reference/serverless-png-azure.jsonc (read its header for how PNG works).
//
// HOW IT WORKS ON DATABRICKS (use this to adapt the diagram to the user's setup):
//   • Lakeflow Connect: define the source connection once in Unity Catalog, then build
//     a fully managed ingestion pipeline on it. Unlike Lakehouse Federation, it runs
//     the transformation on Databricks and reads changes incrementally, so the load on
//     the source stays low. It lands change data in a defined schema and table.
//   • Direct CDC: ONE scheduled serverless pipeline does the snapshot, the CDC and the
//     replication — no separate ingestion gateway to run. Because it is serverless, it
//     needs a serverless path into the network where the database lives.
//   • That path is the Private Network Gateway: a Databricks-managed NIC in a /28 subnet
//     of the hub VNet (delegated to Microsoft.Databricks/workspaces); the pipeline's
//     connection then follows the hub's routes through the VPN / ExpressRoute gateway
//     to the database on-premises. One reusable path instead of a private endpoint and
//     proxy VMs per database.
//   • Limits: CDC mode is in Private Preview and supports SQL Server and Oracle only.
//     During the preview the UI may not create a connection to an on-premises
//     database: create it with SQL. PNG on Azure is in Public Preview, API only.
//
// WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES (layout):
//   • The serverless-png-azure structure with the pipeline as the caller: Databricks box →
//     hub VNet (PNG subnet, gateway subnet) → on-premises. The hops carry `track: "t1"`, so the
//     red path is one straight line.
//   • The red path is the pipeline's connection, drawn in the direction it is opened (pipeline → source);
//     the data comes back on it, so do not draw a second, reversed edge.
//   • The two supported sources are vendor logos in the on-premises box, one edge each;
//     the second leaves the link from its bottom (`er@b` → `oracle@l`) so the two do
//     not fan out of one side and kink.
//   • The preview status lives in the frame subtitle, not in a tile.
//
// ADAPT IT: name the user's databases (one logo and one edge each). If they ask for
// where the data lands, add the Delta tables in Unity Catalog in the Databricks box,
// fed from the pipeline. With no PNG available (or a source that is only reachable
// through a private endpoint service), use the Private Link service pattern instead;
// with no inbound connection allowed at all, use reference/lakeflow-onprem-agent-azure.jsonc.
// =============================================================================
